WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:05.940
During the history of deep learning, many researchers, including some very well-known researchers,

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.100
sometimes proposed optimization algorithms and showed their work well in a few problems.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.320
But those optimization algorithms subsequently were shown not to really generalize that

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:18.380
well to the wide range of neural networks you might want to train.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:23.380
So over time, I think the deep learning community actually developed some amount of skepticism

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:25.500
about new optimization algorithms.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:29.860
And a lot of people felt that gradient descent with momentum really works well.

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:33.180
It's difficult to propose things that work much better.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:38.260
So RMSprop and the Adam optimization algorithm, which I'll talk about in this video, is one

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:44.220
of those rare algorithms that has really stood up and has been shown to work well across a

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:47.200
wide range of deep learning architectures.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.300
So this is one of the algorithms that I wouldn't hesitate to recommend you try because many

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:55.020
people have tried it and seen it work well on many problems.

00:00:55.020 --> 00:01:00.620
And the Adam optimization algorithm is basically taking momentum and RMSprop and putting them

00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:01.620
together.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.380
So let's see how that works.

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:13.060
To implement Adam, you would initialize VdW equals 0, SdW equals 0, and similarly Vdb,

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:16.060
Sdb equals 0.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:26.980
And then on iteration T, you would compute derivatives, compute Vw, Vb using current

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:30.290
mini batch.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:34.570
So usually you do this with mini batch gradient descent.

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:38.930
And then you do the momentum exponentially weighted average.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.610
So VdW equals beta.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:46.610
But now I'm going to call this beta 1 to distinguish it from the hyperparameter beta

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:53.090
2 we'll use for the RMSprop portion of this.

00:01:53.090 --> 00:02:01.290
So this is exactly what we had when we're implementing momentum, except that I've now called the

00:02:01.290 --> 00:02:04.850
hyperparameter beta 1 instead of beta.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:09.810
And similarly, you have Vdb as follows.

00:02:09.810 --> 00:02:15.290
So 1 minus beta 1 times dB.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:18.890
And then you do the RMSprop like update as well.

00:02:18.890 --> 00:02:26.050
So now you have a different hyperparameter beta 2 plus 1 minus beta 2, dW squared.

00:02:26.050 --> 00:02:34.250
And again, the squaring there is element Y squaring of your derivatives dW.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:44.210
And then Sdb is equal to this plus 1 minus beta 2 times dB.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:50.210
So this is the momentum like update with hyperparameter beta 1.

00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:55.730
And this is the RMSprop like update with hyperparameter beta 2.

00:02:55.730 --> 00:03:00.970
In the typical implementation of ADAM, you do implement bias correction.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:04.930
So you're going to have V corrected.

00:03:04.930 --> 00:03:13.290
Corrected means after bias correction, dW equals VdW divided by 1 minus beta 1 to the

00:03:13.290 --> 00:03:14.290
power of t.

00:03:14.290 --> 00:03:23.370
If you've done t iterations and similarly Vdb corrected equals Vdb divided by 1 minus beta

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:25.290
1 to the power of t.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:31.290
And then similarly, you implement this bias correction on S as well.

00:03:31.290 --> 00:03:44.690
So that's SdW divided by 1 minus beta 2 to the t and Sdb corrected equals Sdb divided

00:03:44.690 --> 00:03:48.290
by 1 minus beta 2 to the t.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.890
Finally, you perform the update.

00:03:50.890 --> 00:03:55.410
So W gets updated as W minus alpha times.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:04:03.850
So if you're just implementing momentum, you'd use VdW or maybe VdW corrected.

00:04:03.850 --> 00:04:06.850
But now we add in the RMSprop portion of this.

00:04:06.850 --> 00:04:13.650
So we're also going to divide by square root of SdW corrected plus epsilon.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:18.850
And similarly, B gets updated as a similar formula.

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:29.010
Vdb corrected divided by square root S corrected dB plus epsilon.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:35.210
And so this algorithm combines the effect of gradient descent with momentum together with

00:04:35.210 --> 00:04:37.730
gradient descent with RMSprop.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:43.170
And this is commonly used learning algorithm that's proven to be very effective for many

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:47.010
different neural networks with a very wide variety of architectures.

00:04:47.010 --> 00:04:50.370
So this algorithm has a number of hyperparameters.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:57.530
The learning rate hyperparameter alpha is still important and usually needs to be tuned.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:01.730
So you just have to try a range of values and see what works.

00:05:01.730 --> 00:05:06.330
A common choice, really the default choice for beta 1 is 0.9.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.130
So this is the moving average, weighted average of dW, right?

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:13.050
This is the momentum light term.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:18.810
The hyperparameter for beta 2, the authors of the Adam paper, inventors of the Adam,

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.210
algorithm recommend 0.999.

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:27.050
Again, this is computing the moving weighted average of dW squared as well as dB squared.

00:05:27.050 --> 00:05:31.250
And then epsilon, the choice of epsilon doesn't matter very much.

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:34.970
But the authors of the Adam paper recommend it 10 to the minus 8.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:40.850
But this parameter, you really don't need to set it and it doesn't affect performance

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:43.010
much at all.

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:47.570
But when implementing Adam, what people usually do is just use the default values of beta

00:05:47.570 --> 00:05:50.010
1 and beta 2 as well as epsilon.

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.730
I don't think anyone ever really tunes epsilon.

00:05:52.730 --> 00:05:56.890
And then try a range of values of alpha to see what works best.

00:05:56.890 --> 00:06:01.570
You could also tune beta 1 and beta 2, but it's not done that often among the practitioners

00:06:01.570 --> 00:06:02.970
as I know.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:06.450
So where does the term Adam come from?

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:15.660
Adam stands for adaptive moment estimation.

00:06:15.660 --> 00:06:18.340
So beta 1 is computing the mean of the derivatives.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:20.100
This is called the first moment.

00:06:20.100 --> 00:06:24.340
And beta 2 is used to compute exponentially weighted average of the squares.

00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:26.020
And that's called the second moment.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:29.620
So that gives rise to the name adaptive moment estimation.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:33.180
But everyone just calls it the Adam optimization algorithm.

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:37.660
And by the way, one of my long term friends and collaborators is called Adam Coates.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:41.740
As far as I know, this algorithm doesn't have anything to do with him except for the fact

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:43.580
that I think he uses it sometimes.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:45.260
But sometimes I get asked that question.

00:06:45.260 --> 00:06:48.260
So just in case you're wondering.

00:06:48.260 --> 00:06:50.940
So that's it for the Adam optimization algorithm.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:54.820
With it, I think you really train your neural networks much more quickly.

00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:59.420
But before we wrap up for this week, let's keep talking about hyperparameter tuning as

00:06:59.420 --> 00:07:03.340
well as gain somewhat intuitions about what the optimization problem for neural

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:04.340
networks looks like.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.300
In the next video, we'll talk about learning rate decay.
